# K8s integration: architecture and request/response flows

This PR covers components, how to run, HTTP APIs, lifecycle flows, scaling behaviors, inventory management, and supporting scripts/protos.

## Overview
- The k8s feature manages the full lifecycle of deployments on a Kubernetes cluster: manifests → workloads → monitoring → expiry/teardown, with service-aware scale-to-zero and scale-back.
- HTTP endpoints are mounted under `/k8s` via `core/corehttp/gateway.go` when `n.K8sDeployer` is enabled.

## How to run
- Provide `bid_market` & `provider` contract address. Enable `bidengine`, `vpn` & `deployer` too. Make sure the kubeconfig file is located in `kubeconfig_path`:
```yaml
contracts:
  bid_market: "0xbf5cB24B533fF73836024A5a4F5a033670dc9858"
  provider: "0xD4413ea0b2F525451E7a40bdd50010A2c2Fc9FBA"
bidengine:
  enable: true
  provider_id: 1
vpn:
  enable: true
  mtu: 9000
  virtual_ip: 10.0.0.2
deployer:
  enable: true
  kubeconfig_path: "~/.kube/config"
```
- Setup K8s objects & sub-services: run `make kube-setup`
- Run the `subnet-node` 
- See the cluster & inventory status in `GET /k8s/api/v1/status`
- Try requesting a deployment on an active lease (an order has `OrderStatusAccepted` status on `bid_market` contract) on `POST /k8s/api/v1/deployments`
